In mathematics, particularly in differential geometry, an osculating plane is a plane in a Euclidean space or affine space which meets a submanifold at a point in such a way as to have a second order of contact at the point. 
The word osculate is from Latin osculari 'to kiss'; an osculating plane is thus a plane which "kisses" a submanifold. 

The osculating plane in the geometry of Euclidean space curves can be described in terms of the Frenet-Serret formulas as the linear span of the tangent and normal vectors. 





--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The osculating plane at a point on a space curve is the plane that most closely fits the curve near that point. 
It is spanned by the curve's unit tangent vector and unit pricipal normal vector, and it contains the curvature vector. 
Given a smooth space curve r(t) with nonzero curvature at a point r(t₀), the osculating plane is the plane through r(t₀) with normal vector B(t₀) = T(t₀) X N(t₀), where T is the unit tangent vector and N is the unit pricipal normal vector. 
Equivalently, it is spanned by r'(t₀) and r''(t₀), provided they are linearly independent. 
